================================================================================
CROSS-CUTTING INVARIANT — identity/storage rule for anything CX-backed
(applies to samples, plugins, params, and any future module that gets CX
representation - not params-specific, written down here because the params
resync work is what forced it to be stated explicitly)
================================================================================
The CX layer resolves a ContextId to a module's raw user_data pointer ONCE
and reuses that SAME pointer across many later calls, without re-resolving
it each time (see the Phase D note this task_list used to carry: "CX caches
data_id, reconcile never re-resolves it"). This is already how samples and
plugins work today, not a new rule invented for params:
  sampler_child_at(): out->user_data = smp;   // raw pointer into SMP_INFO's
                                                // fixed array
  smp_sample_return(): "the samples array can have gaps, walk the occupied
                        slots in order and return the idx-th one" - a
                        verbatim comment already in contexts/sampler.c
So: a fixed-array-backed module must NEVER move a surviving item's storage
once a ContextId has been handed out for it (no compaction on remove - free
slots become gaps/tombstones, reused only by a later NEW item, which gets a
new identity). This holds regardless of threading - it is a hazard even
fully single-threaded, because the cache persists across frames/calls, not
because of concurrent access. Params.c's own add/remove-by-uid design
(STAGE 2 below) follows this same rule for exactly this reason - it is not
a params-specific invention, it is bringing params in line with what
samples/plugins already rely on.
The only real alternative - cache the stable key only, resolve key ->
current live slot fresh on every single op call (dead -> NULL) instead of
caching a raw pointer - would make compaction safe, but it is a change to
app_intrf.c's fundamental CX-caching strategy, not a per-module one; it
trades an O(1) dereference for an O(N) lookup on every op call system-wide;
and every existing module would need to switch for consistency, not just
whichever one is being worked on that day. Not worth it unless something
concrete demands it later - noted here so the tradeoff doesn't need
re-deriving next time a new CX-backed module comes up.

