STAGE 1 - replace params.c's own value-building and display formatting with
owner callbacks. TWO separate hooks, not one - they have different
audiences and one of them is RT-constrained:

  (a) value-building callback (numeric, feeds param_get_value - RT/DSP path)
  Current state: param_get_value(container, val_id, curved, interp,
  rt_params) does the value-building itself - if curved, normalizes into
  0..1 (fit_range) and runs it through PRM_PARAM.curve_table
  (math_range_table_convert_value), then maps back to range; if interp,
  feeds the result through params_interp_val_get_value (PRM_PARAM.
  interp_val). Generic, flag-driven math params.c owns internally.
  param_get_value is called directly by RT/DSP code, not just for display -
  e.g. synth.c:611 `param_get_value(osc->params, 6, 1, 0, 1)` (curved=1,
  rt_params=1: an ADSR attack time read on the AUDIO THREAD, curve-mapped,
  fed straight into synthesis math), same pattern for plugins.c/
  clap_plugins.c feeding an LV2/CLAP control port. So param_get_value
  MUST keep returning PARAM_T - a string return here would break real DSP
  (the audio thread can't parse a string back into a float, and RT code
  shouldn't be formatting strings anyway). Goal: params.c stops doing the
  curved/interp math itself; the owner (sample/plugin/synth osc - whoever
  calls params_init_param_container) supplies a callback in
  PRM_CONT_USER_DATA that builds the final PARAM_T from the raw stored one
  - curve mapping, interpolation, anything else the owner wants - params.c
  just calls it. Exact signature/whether curve_table + interp_val stay as
  PRM_PARAM-owned storage or move entirely into the owner's own callback
  state is not decided yet.

  (b) display callback (string, replaces param_get_value_as_string - UI-only)
  Current state: param_get_value_as_string(container, val_id, ...) - its own
  doc comment already says "Use only on [main-thread] for ui display
  purposes," so no RT constraint here - has its own val_type switch
  (Uchar_type/Int_type/Float_type/DB_Return_Type/Curve_Float_Return_Type/
  String_Return_Type) building the display string, with PRM_CONT_USER_DATA.
  val_to_string only as an optional first-look override, not the only path.
  Goal [DECIDED]: the owner's callback becomes the ONLY path - it decides
  whether this parameter displays as a plain float, a dB readout, a
  True/False toggle, a choice-list label, anything else, and params.c stops
  knowing any of those categories exist - the val_type switch goes away
  entirely, not just gets a bypass.
  Consequence [DECIDED]: PRM_PARAM.param_strings / param_set_param_strings /
  param_get_param_string disappear too - that whole mechanism only existed
  to let String_Return_Type params look up a display label by indexing into
  a params.c-owned string array; once the owner's display callback decides
  formatting entirely, the owner just holds its own choice labels and
  indexes into them itself inside the callback, same as it already
  effectively does today, just moved out of params.c. Whether PRM_PARAM.
  val_type itself has any remaining reason to exist once neither function
  branches on it is an open question - check during this stage, don't
  assume yet.

  (c) per-parameter uid becomes mandatory [DECIDED]
  Checked first: grep for `.user_id =` across contexts/*.c shows only
  clap_plugins.c ever sets PRM_USER_DATA.user_id to something real
  (param_info.id, CLAP's own clap_id). plugins.c (LV2), synth.c, sampler.c
  never touch it - it silently defaults to 0 for every one of their params
  today. So this is establishing a new cross-module contract, not
  formalising an existing one - real work needed in those three modules
  too, not just documentation.
  Why mandatory: once a param becomes a CX node (STAGE 3), its
  ContextId/DataChoice.value has to be MAKE_ID(ns, uid) - an owner that
  leaves uid at the default 0 doesn't get a "less good" id, every one of
  that owner's params collides on the exact same key. This isn't about
  assuming every owner can dynamically add/remove params (most won't) -
  ANY param exposed as a CX node needs stable identity regardless of
  whether its owner's set ever changes shape. What dynamism (CLAP's
  RESCAN_ALL, see STAGE 2) actually buys is the justification for one
  GENERIC diff-based add/remove mechanism instead of two different ones
  (static init vs dynamic resync) - for a static owner (synth/sampler/Trk)
  the diff always comes back empty, so it costs them nothing to ride the
  same mechanism.
  Scope: only needs to be stable+unique WITHIN one owner's own container,
  same guarantee clap_id itself actually gives (unique per plugin instance,
  not globally) - global uniqueness is composed one level up by the CX
  layer combining this with the owning instance's own uid, same "compose
  an owner id into the local part" pattern already used for per-instance
  lists (see IDENTITY/KEYS notes from the original actions-system design).
  For synth/sampler/Trk, which have no external-format id to reuse, their
  own existing fixed array position (0=Amp, 1=Freq, ...) already satisfies
  this - no burden, since those sets never change shape.
  API shape [DECIDED]: don't leave it inside the already-nullable
  PRM_USER_DATA* user_data_per_param (bundling a mandatory field inside an
  optional-as-a-whole pointer is contradictory). Pull it into its own
  required parameter on params_init_param_container, parallel to
  param_names/param_vals (which already return NULL if missing - same
  validation style, just extended to cover uid). Leave PRM_USER_DATA.data
  (the CLAP cookie, genuinely optional/module-specific) where it is.
  params_init_param_container should also reject duplicate uids at init
  time (cheap linear scan, param counts are tens not thousands) so a
  broken owner fails loudly there instead of corrupting the CX layer
  later - same idea extended into STAGE 2's resync function.
  Minor, not urgent: clap_id is uint32_t; today's user_id is a signed int -
  worth tightening the type once this is load-bearing.

  Not decided yet, for whenever this stage actually starts: exact value/
  display callback signatures, and going through params.c function by
  function to see what else follows from these changes.

STAGE 2 - deferred cleanup (explicitly "later", not blocking stage 1 or 3)
  - Allocation count: e.g. params_init_param_container mallocs rt/ui
    interp_val separately per parameter per side. (param_set_param_strings'
    own per-string mallocs go away with stage 1's string-function removal,
    not a stage 2 concern any more.) Worth a pass once the callback change
    (stage 1) settles what state actually needs to live in PRM_PARAM at all.
  - Parameters added/removed at runtime by a plugin [DESIGN DECIDED,
    triggered by a concrete existing case - see below]. Today
    num_of_params_ui/_rt and the rt_params/ui_params arrays are sized once
    in params_init_param_container (calloc) and never resized.

    The concrete forcing case: clap_plug_ext_params_rescan
    (contexts/clap_plugins.c) already exists and already handles
    CLAP_PARAM_RESCAN_VALUES/TEXT/INFO for real (those only touch EXISTING
    params by position, never add/remove, so they're unaffected by any of
    this). Its CLAP_PARAM_RESCAN_ALL branch - the one that means "the
    param count/order/ids changed" - is a TODO with the naive approach
    commented out: `clap_plug_params_destroy(...); clap_plug_params_create(
    ...);`, i.e. destroy the whole container and rebuild from scratch. That
    would give every surviving param a brand-new ContextId even if it
    didn't actually change - breaks the CROSS-CUTTING INVARIANT above and
    breaks any UI_STATE/UI reference pointed at a param that's still
    logically the same one. The old TODO comment ("need to overhaul the
    whole app_intrf for this") is already resolved by the actions/CX system
    that now exists - no app_intrf overhaul needed, see STAGE 3's plumbing
    notes for exactly what app_intrf does and doesn't need.

    Algorithm [DECIDED] - params_container_resync(container, new_params[],
    new_count), called by the owner (CLAP's RESCAN_ALL handler today, any
    future dynamic-params owner later) with its CURRENT full param list,
    matched by uid not position, per the CROSS-CUTTING INVARIANT (tombstone,
    never compact a surviving slot):
      pass 1 - removals: for each currently-alive slot whose uid is no
        longer present in new_params: release any owned state, mark the
        slot dead (tombstoned), do NOT shift anything.
      pass 2 - additions: for each new_params[i] whose uid has no matching
        alive slot: reuse a tombstoned slot if one exists, else grow
        rt_params/ui_params by one (realloc); populate it exactly like
        params_init_param_container already does per-param today (name,
        min, max, inc, val_type, def_val, val=def_val, uid, just_changed=0,
        alive=true).
      survivors (uid found in both): untouched entirely - not visited by
        either pass. Value/name refresh for survivors is RESCAN_VALUES/
        INFO's job (already implemented, separate from this), not this
        function's.
      Both rt_params and ui_params get identical treatment in lockstep,
      same slot index in both, mirroring params_init_param_container's
      existing symmetry. Safe to mutate both directly (no ring-buffer
      messaging needed for the structural part) because CLAP only allows
      RESCAN_ALL while the plugin instance is deactivated (the existing
      `if (plug->plug_inst_activated == 1) return;` guard already in
      clap_plug_ext_params_rescan enforces this) - the RT thread isn't
      reading rt_params for that plugin while this runs.
      This function does NOT report what changed to anyone - it only
      leaves the container in the correct new state. STAGE 3's CX resync
      independently discovers what actually changed later, by re-walking
      child_at and diffing against what it already has - same as it
      already does for every other container. Two separate diffs (this
      one, and the CX one), not one handed from one side to the other.
    Needs a uid-lookup helper (param_find_uid, mirroring the existing
    param_find_name's linear scan, same reasoning - counts are small).
    Array growth: realloc to exact size on demand, not doubling-growth -
    rescans are a rare/cold event here, not a hot interactive-append path
    like the CLI's UI_TARGET_LIST, so the simpler exact-size realloc is
    fine and not premature optimisation avoidance.
  - Redundant code: nearly every accessor in params.c repeats the same
    `if (rt_params == 0) {array=ui_params; ...} if (rt_params == 1)
    {array=rt_params; ...}` array-select block - a clear factor-out
    candidate (see no-parallel-signature-twins memory for the general
    preference this would follow).
  - Re-check against the action system once stage 3 exists: some of this
    cleanup may turn out to be required by stage 3 rather than independent
    - revisit the split once stage 3's shape is clearer.

STAGE 3 - parameters as contexts (CX), integrated into the action system
  Agreed direction: yes, parameters should be CX tree nodes, not a live
  external list like JACK ports. The reasoning that settled ports as
  NOT contexts was that they are externally driven and churny (hotplug,
  processes outside this program). Parameters are the opposite - owned and
  defined entirely by this app's own modules, stable for the lifetime of
  their owning sample/plugin instance/synth osc, exactly like the loaded
  plugin instances that already are CX children. So parameters should be
  browsable/actionable the same way samples and plugins already are.
  Open questions to settle before building (not decided):
  - Materialize every parameter as a CX child of its owning context, or
    only non-hidden ones (PRM_PARAM.is_hidden already exists)? A plugin can
    have a lot of params (tens to low hundreds) - worth checking real
    numbers against how the rest of the CX tree scales before committing.
  - Value changes go through param_set_value's existing rt_params=0 (ui
    side) path and its ring-buffer message to the rt thread - this already
    matches the pattern the rest of the app uses (JACK, plugins), shouldn't
    need new plumbing, just confirm during implementation.
  - How params become CX children, and how structural changes (RESCAN_ALL)
    reach app_intrf [DECIDED - reuses the existing generic pipeline,
    verified against actual code, not assumed]:
      app_intrf's resync (enumerate a parent's current children via its
      ops table, diff against the CX tree, emit CX_ADDED/CX_REMOVED) is
      ALREADY fully generic - it doesn't know or care what kind of child
      it's looking at. It needs ZERO new code for params. The "structural
      change happened" signal is NOT a new DataEvent type either - it's
      the exact same DATA_EVENT_CHILDREN_CHANGED already used for "a
      plugin was added to the plugins list", just now ALSO fired targeting
      a plugin/synth-osc/sample INSTANCE's own ContextId (not only
      container-list ContextIds).
      What's actually new, checked directly against app_data.c: every
      item-level ops table - sample_ops, lv2_plugin_ops, clap_plugin_ops,
      the synth osc's ops - currently stubs child_at to unconditionally
      `return false;` (`(void)user_data; (void)idx; (void)out; return
      false;`). They are hardcoded leaves today, not "almost wired up".
      Giving them real param children means implementing REAL child_at/
      child_count there, backed by PRM_CONTAIN (once STAGE 2 lands).
      Each owning instance needs its own params_dirty bool (NOT the
      existing container-level plugins_dirty/samples_dirty - those mean
      "the SET of loaded instances changed"; params_dirty is instance-
      scoped, since one plugin's RESCAN_ALL says nothing about another's),
      set by the RESCAN_ALL handler right after params_container_resync
      finishes (mutate first, flag second).
      That flag doesn't need a new iteration to check it either -
      clap_read_rt_to_ui_messages (already called every cycle from
      app_data_update) already loops every currently-loaded CLAP_PLUG_PLUG
      to drain each instance's param_msgs_process. Checking
      cur_plug->params_dirty and pushing DATA_EVENT_CHILDREN_CHANGED for
      that instance's ContextId slots into that same existing loop - one
      more check per iteration, not a second pass. (Needs to loop, not be
      a single flag check like JACK's ports_changed - several loaded
      plugins could rescan in the same cycle, unlike JACK which is one
      client.) LV2/synth/sampler would need the equivalent read function
      to grow the same kind of check, whenever/if any of them gain a
      dynamic-params story of their own.
  - DataActionType(s) for changing a value [DECIDED]: two, not three, not
    one. data_actions.h already anticipates this - DataActionType has a
    "// rename / value-change slot in later" comment, and DataArgKind has a
    commented-out "// DATA_ARG_INT, DATA_ARG_DOUBLE later".
      DATA_ACTION_SET_VALUE    - one numeric arg, the exact value to set.
      DATA_ACTION_ADJUST_VALUE - one small signed numeric arg, a STEP COUNT
                                 (not a raw value) - action_do interprets it
                                 in the owner's own step size, e.g.
                                 param_set_value(..., Operation_Increase or
                                 Operation_Decrease depending on sign,
                                 rt_params=0).
    Rejected: three separate actions (SET/INCREASE/DECREASE) - increase and
    decrease are the same operation with an opposite sign, no reason to
    double the enum for that. Also rejected: SET_VALUE only, with the CLI
    computing current+increment itself for a no-prompt +/- shortcut - what
    one step means is owner-defined behaviour (clamping today, possibly
    curve/owner-callback-aware after stage 1), not something the UI should
    reconstruct from raw metadata even if min/max/step were exposed on
    DataArgSpec for display purposes - same reasoning REMOVE/ADD_CHOICE
    were already built on: UI triggers, owner decides the domain-specific
    part. A no-prompt CLI +/- shortcut still works fine under
    ADJUST_VALUE: the shortcut handler builds the DataActionReq itself
    (bypassing the normal arg-prompt flow, same as any other direct-invoke
    shortcut - see the CLI +/- key note above) and always sends step=+1/-1;
    a future scroll-wheel or held-key-repeat UI can send a larger step
    count through the exact same action, no new DataActionType needed.
    Implementation cost is trivial either way - params.c's
    param_set_value_directly already implements every one of these
    operations (Operation_SetValue/Increase/Decrease/...); action_do for
    both new types is one line each into the existing param_set_value(...,
    rt_params=0) call, no new params.c logic.
    Other paramOperType values (DefValue/SetIncr/SetDefValue/ChangeName/
    ToggleHidden) stay unexposed to the action system for now - add one
    enum value + one ops-table case each only once something actually
    needs it (same "explicit over clever" extensibility rule the rest of
    the action system was built on).

