FUTURE - categories for CHOICE / MULTI_CHOICE lists (design sketch, not
scheduled - written down now because plugin presets will need it: preset
browsers are essentially always categorised, e.g. LV2 preset banks/CLAP
preset factories exposing a category/collection hierarchy)
================================================================================
  No new DataListId / DataArgKind / DataActionType needed - a category-
  bearing list is still just a flat, idx-enumerable list_at, exactly like
  today. Two additions to data_actions.h cover it:
  - DataChoice gains a second flags bit: DATA_CHOICE_CATEGORY (1u << 1),
    alongside the existing DATA_CHOICE_LINKED (1u << 0). A row with this bit
    set is a node to descend into, not a pickable leaf - same idea as a
    directory listing mixing files and subdirectories in one flat readdir,
    "is this a directory" is a flag on the entry, not a different call.
  - DataActionReq gains a top-level field, sibling to `type`, OUTSIDE the
    action-specific union: `uint64_t category_scope;` (MAKE_ID(list_ns,
    category key), 0 = list root). Set by the UI while drilling a
    category-bearing list; ignored by list_at for lists that have no
    categories; never meaningful in the req that finally reaches action_do
    - only the union fields (add_choice/connect/...) are, so no ops table's
    action_do needs to change for this to exist.
    [Correction from an earlier version of this idea: originally proposed
    reusing add_choice.choice_value itself as the drill scope while
    browsing (elegant for ADD_CHOICE's single list, since nothing else
    needs that field mid-browse) - doesn't generalise. CONNECT's targets
    list is already scoped by partial->connect.source, which has to stay
    the fixed, correct source for the whole browsing session; a second,
    independent "which category am I inside" value has no spare field to
    live in without colliding with source. A dedicated top-level field
    avoids the collision and works identically for every action shape.]

  How drilling works, CLI side: one more branch in the existing "on
  SELECTED" handling already used by helper_choice_list_step's callers, not
  a new architecture -
    - pick-and-return shape (CONNECT's SOURCE mode, a plain non-repeating
      CHOICE): category row -> update category_scope, reset cursor, browse
      the same list again; leaf row -> return, exactly as today.
    - act-and-continue shape (ADD_CHOICE's repeat, CONNECT's TARGETS
      toggle): category row -> descend, same as above; leaf row -> act on
      it (execute / toggle) and keep looping at the SAME scope, exactly as
      today.
  Backing out (h/ESC) needs a small caller-owned breadcrumb: a fixed-size
  stack of (scope value, cursor) pairs, one per level drilled into, capped
  like ACTION_ARG_COUNT/MAX_ACTION_CANDIDATES already are elsewhere in this
  CLI - no heap. Pop one level per h/ESC (cursor restored, same as CONNECT's
  source_cursor preservation) until back at the root, where it behaves like
  today (leaves the picker / steps back to the outer mode).

  Two things list_at implementations need to get right, whenever this is
  actually built:
  - A category node's own DataChoice.value must hash its FULL PATH (e.g.
    fnv1a64("Bass/Analog/Warm")), not just its own name - otherwise two
    different parents with a same-named child (Bass/Warm vs Lead/Warm)
    collide. Leaf items keep using whatever globally-unique identity they
    already have (a preset URI/path) - category_scope only filters which
    leaves are visible at a given level, it is never folded into a leaf's
    own identity.
  - Only the immediate parent's scope value is ever needed by list_at (not
    the whole ancestor chain) - a category node's children are determined
    by the node itself, same reasoning already established for CONNECT's
    one-level source/target dependency. The CLI's own breadcrumb stack is
    for backing out visually, not something list_at needs to see.

  Rejected alternatives:
  - A dedicated new DataArgKind / a list_children-style call for tree
    enumeration - not a genuinely new interaction shape (still "pick from
    an enumerable list", just applied recursively), so a new contract
    surface would violate the "a new arg kind is the only thing that
    should touch the UI" rule the rest of the action system was built on.
  - Returning a nested tree from one list_at call - breaks the flat-
    pagination contract every existing caller relies on, and would need
    heap-allocated variable-depth structures crossing data_actions.h's
    dependency-free boundary. The flag-plus-drill-loop keeps everything
    flat and caller-stack-owned, matching "all interface buffers are
    caller stack" already established for the rest of this system.

  Checked for robustness against MULTI_CHOICE and CONNECT specifically
  (not just the single-list ADD_CHOICE case) before writing this down -
  category_scope as a top-level field (not a reused union field) is what
  makes it compose with CONNECT's existing source/target scoping without
  collision; see the correction note above.
